(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the core internal architecture of Pandas Series and DataFrames, detailing index alignment and columnar memory layout.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Architecture Overview:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Pandas Series:) Tj
T*
(   A Series is a 1-dimensional labeled array composed of two primary synchronized arrays:) Tj
T*
(   - A NumPy `ndarray` \(or Arrow chunked array\) storing homogeneous data values.) Tj
T*
(   - An `Index` object storing immutable axis labels \(integers, strings, or timestamps\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Pandas DataFrame:) Tj
T*
(   A DataFrame is a 2-dimensional tabular data structure with heterogeneous column data types.) Tj
T*
(   Internally, columns are managed by a columnar block manager where columns of identical dtypes) Tj
T*
(   are grouped into contiguous 2D NumPy arrays.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Automatic Index Alignment:) Tj
T*
(   The defining engineering feature of Pandas is automatic index alignment during operations. When) Tj
T*
(   performing arithmetic between two Series: `s1 + s2`, Pandas does not blindly add elements at) Tj
T*
(   identical physical integer offsets. Instead, it performs an outer union on their labels,) Tj
T*
(   matching identical keys and populating `NaN` for non-matching labels, preventing silent data) Tj
T*
(   misalignments in asynchronous telemetry feeds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Contrast label-based indexing `.loc` with integer-position-based indexing `.iloc` in Pandas with boundary condition examples.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Indexing Comparison:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. `.loc` \(Label-Based Indexing\):) Tj
T*
(   - Selects rows and columns based on index and column names/labels.) Tj
T*
(   - Slicing is INCLUSIVE of both the start and the stop label:) Tj
T*
(   `df.loc['2026-01-01':'2026-01-05']` includes data from January 5th!) Tj
T*
(   - Supports boolean series masks: `df.loc[df['voltage'] > 3.3, ['ch1', 'ch2']]`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. `.iloc` \(Integer-Position-Based Indexing\):) Tj
T*
(   - Selects rows and columns based on zero-based integer physical array offsets \(0 to N-1\).) Tj
T*
(   - Slicing follows standard Python semi-open interval semantics: EXCLUSIVE of the stop boundary:) Tj
T*
(   `df.iloc[0:5]` selects exactly rows 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 \(5 rows total\).) Tj
T*
(   - Rejects boolean series if their indices do not match integer positions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the `SettingWithCopyWarning` in Pandas: root causes, memory hazards, and the Copy-on-Write \(CoW\) solution in Pandas 2.0+.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Root Cause of `SettingWithCopyWarning`:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   This warning occurs when performing chained indexing assignment:) Tj
T*
(   `df[df['ch'] == 1]['voltage'] = 3.3`) Tj
T*
(   In this code, `df[df['ch'] == 1]` is evaluated first. Pandas may return either an in-memory) Tj
T*
ET

