(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the object-oriented architecture of Matplotlib: Figure, Axes, Axis, Tick, and Artist hierarchy.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Matplotlib Object-Oriented Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. `Figure`:) Tj
T*
(   The top-level container that represents the entire window or page canvas. It manages subplots,) Tj
T*
(   global figure titles \(`suptitle`\), and overall dimensions \(`figsize`\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. `Axes`:) Tj
T*
(   The actual plotting area or subplot coordinate system. An Axes object contains:) Tj
T*
(   - Two \(or three in 3D\) `Axis` objects that regulate coordinate limits, ticks, and tick labels.) Tj
T*
(   - Plot title, x/y labels, legends, and gridlines.) Tj
T*
(   - All drawn graphical `Artists` \(lines, bars, text, polygons\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. `Artist` Base Class:) Tj
T*
(   Everything visible on the canvas is an `Artist` \(e.g. `Line2D`, `Rectangle`, `Circle`, `Text`\).) Tj
T*
(   Rendering proceeds by traversing the Artist tree and calling each artist's `draw\(renderer\)`) Tj
T*
(   method onto the backend canvas.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
